# Base rules

## Doc map

- **Setup & commands** → [README.md](../../README.md)
- **System design & deployment** → [ARCHITECTURE.md](../../ARCHITECTURE.md)
- **Stack, paths & conventions** → [PROJECT.md](../../PROJECT.md)

## TypeScript

- No `enum`; use a `const` object or string union instead.
- No `any` without a short inline comment explaining why.
- Prefer explicit return types on exported functions.
- Prefer `unknown` over `any` for truly unknown external data; narrow with a type guard.

## Functions & naming

- Prefer arrow functions for handlers, helpers, and callbacks.
- No single-letter names except loop indices (`i`, `j`).
- Use `event` (not `e`) for DOM event parameters.
- Named handlers for non-trivial logic; one-line inline handlers are OK for trivial wiring.

## File size & structure

- ≤ ~120 lines per file (soft cap); split by responsibility when you exceed it.
- One concept per file — don't bundle unrelated utilities.

## Scope of changes

- Do not add features, refactor code, or make "improvements" beyond what was asked.
- Don't add docstrings, comments, or type annotations to code you didn't change.
- Don't add error handling or fallbacks for scenarios that can't happen.
- Validate only at system boundaries (user input, external APIs) — not deep inside pure logic.
- Don't create helpers or abstractions for one-time use.

## Comments

- Add comments only where the logic isn't self-evident.
- No redundant comments that restate what the code does.

## Effort & token efficiency

- Read PROJECT.md first; then only files relevant to the task.
- Prefer a single targeted `Grep` or `Glob` over broad exploration.
- Batch independent tool calls in parallel.

## Repo hygiene

- Run `npm run fix` before pushing: ESLint `--fix`, then Prettier.
